import std/io

// A map destructuring whose key is ABSENT — the one place this construct
// produces observable output of its own.
//
// It is a TRAP and not an assertion failure, and that is the distinction worth
// pinning: `assert {"a" => v} = m` would report through the assertion channel,
// while `{"a" => v} = m` goes through applyDestructure and faults with
// `line N: key K not found in map`. The two exits are different and the
// statement forms sit next to each other in stmtInto, so nothing but a test
// keeps them apart.
//
// The key is rendered by `Display`, not by `Debug` — `keyVal.Display()` on the
// interpreter side — so a String key prints BARE with no quotes. A VM path
// that reached for the inspect spelling would produce `"z"` here and agree on
// everything else.
//
// The missing key is a SIDE-EFFECTING EXPRESSION, and that is the second thing
// this fixture pins. The fault text needs the key rendered, so the key is read
// twice — once by the lookup and once by the report — and a lowering that
// spelled the expression at both sites would print `evaluating z` TWICE on a
// path the interpreter walks once. That divergence is invisible on any
// destructuring that HITS, because the report lives inside the miss branch, so
// it can only be caught here.
//
// Both paths must produce the same partial stdout, the same error text and exit
// 1, and the text has ONE implementation (rt.MapKeyMissingError, which eval
// calls down into) so a test comparing two copies cannot pass while they drift.
fn main() {
    m = {"a" => 1, "b" => 2}
    {note("a") => present} = m
    io.print("present = ${present}")
    {note("z") => absent} = m
    io.print("unreachable ${absent}")
}

fn note(s: String): String {
    io.print("evaluating ${s}")
    s
}
